FAQ = [
 ("Do 2015–2022 Colorado tonneau covers fit a 2023 Colorado?",
  "Assume not. The major brands sell separate part numbers for the third-generation truck: BAKFlip MX4 448126 is for 2015–2022 and 448146 for 2023+, RetraxPRO MX 80454 stops at 2022, and TruXedo's Lo Pro 549801 is listed for 2015–2022 only. A few sellers list one cover for 2015–2026. Rough Country does this for its hard tri-fold, for example. Those can be right, but confirm with the seller that the part was checked on a 2023+ bed."),
 ("What size bed does the 2023+ Colorado have?",
  "One size. Every 2023+ Colorado is a Crew Cab with a short box, and Chevrolet's brochure lists the floor length at 61.7 in for all models. Cover makers label it differently: BAK calls it 5'2\" (62 in), Tyger 5'1\" (61 in), and Retrax and Rough Country 5 ft. These are all the same bed. The extended cab and 6'2\" long bed ended with the 2022 model year."),
 ("Will a tonneau cover fit a Colorado Trail Boss?",
  "Check each cover. Rough Country states that both its Colorado hard roll-up and its hard tri-fold do not fit Trail Boss models because of interference with the factory sport bar. Owners on ColoradoFans have a thread specifically about covers that clear the sport bar on Trail Boss, ZR2 and Z71 short beds. If your truck has a sport bar, ask the seller about it before ordering any cover."),
 ("Does a cover work with the StowFlex tailgate?",
  "The StowFlex tailgate has a lockable 45 in wide, 4 in deep compartment built into the gate, standard on the 2023 ZR2 and optional on other trims. Tonneau covers seal on top of the tailgate, and the compartment's lid opens with the tailgate lowered, so there's no obvious conflict. None of the makers here mention StowFlex specifically, though, so confirm with the seller if you use the compartment every day."),
 ("Does the GMC Canyon use the same covers?",
  "Yes. The 2023+ Canyon shares the Colorado's bed, and every part number on this page is sold as Colorado/Canyon: BAKFlip MX4 448146, RetraxONE MX 60455, Gator FX 8828146 and TruXedo Pro X15 TS 1250016. The same rule applies: 2015–2022 Canyon covers are different part numbers. If a listing names only the Canyon or only the Colorado, check the other brand's fitment on the maker's own site. The bed dimensions are shared, so the part number is identical either way."),
 ("What's the best tonneau cover for a 2023+ Colorado?",
  "For most owners, the BAKFlip MX4 448146. RealTruck lists it at $1,049.99 with a 400 lb distributed load rating, a 5-year warranty and a sub-30-minute install, and it folds against the cab for full bed access without blocking the third brake light. If you want a hard cover that rolls, and you don't have a Trail Boss, Rough Country's hard roll-up is rated 600 lb for $749.95."),
 ("Can I put a bed rack on a Colorado with a tonneau cover?",
  "Yes, if you pick the cover for it. The TruXedo Pro X15 TS has integrated T-slot rails designed for racks, cargo holders and tents, and TruXedo sells it bundled with its Elevate rack. Retrax also sells a RetraxPRO XR (T-80455) for this truck with T-slot rails. A standard folding cover leaves nothing to mount a rack to."),
 ("Soft or hard cover for a midsize truck like the Colorado?",
  "Hard covers here carry 200–600 lb spread evenly and lock under a locked tailgate. The smaller Colorado bed makes a folded tri-fold's lost space more noticeable, which is why a fold-to-cab design like the MX4 or a roll-up suits it well. Soft covers ($229–$570 here) are lighter, faster to remove and cheaper, but vinyl only keeps weather and eyes out."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install one?",
  "No. Every cover here clamps inside the bed rails. The makers quote 5–10 minutes for the Tyger T3, 30 minutes or less for the MX4 and Gator FX, 30–45 minutes for Rough Country, and up to an hour for the RetraxONE and TruXedo. One common snag: Tyger says an over-the-rail bedliner needs small holes cut for the clamps. Under-rail liners need nothing."),
]

